%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
title: b3.1 Settling in, meeting and trust
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Eleven concepts. Why a walk may be one activity suggestion: time outdoors and on foot may lift mood, though the evidence in dementia is weak (HFC-01). Why newcomers withdraw: moving in is a loss, and adjusting takes months (HFC-02). Why residents do not meet on their own: each waits for the other (HFC-16), refusing a request is hard (HFC-17), and residents misread each other's interest (HFC-05). What brings them together: a shared background (HFC-15) and common ground (HFC-04). How talk survives a slip: self-repair first (HFC-03). What to protect: face (HFC-06). What withdrawal costs: loneliness (HFC-07). Why no deception: trust (HFC-08).

= Human Factors Concept - Fresh air, movement and mood [HFC-01] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-01
|Name|Fresh air, movement and mood
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only. Evidence in dementia is weak (O-1).
|Concept statement|Time outdoors and on foot may lift mood and help people keep up daily activities. Side by side, on the move, talk may come more easily and a pause may cost less. For these reasons a walk can be one optional activity where both residents prefer it and are assessed fit; the evidence does not justify making it the default.
|Human factor addressed|Mood; daily functioning; conversation
|Proposed mechanism|Exercise maintains physical function. Time in nature lowers rumination. Walking side by side asks for no eye contact, and the surroundings give talk a subject.
|Source|Forbes et al. (2015), Cochrane review: in dementia, promising evidence that exercise helps with daily activities; no clear effect on cognition, mood or behaviour; evidence of very low quality. Bratman et al. (2015): a nature walk lowered rumination in healthy adults. Evans & Jones (2011): walking interviews draw more talk about place.
|Conditions and population of validity|Mostly healthy adults; evidence in dementia weak and mixed.
|Predicted Effect type|More pleasure in time spent together; calmer afterwards
|Associated Measures|M-03, M-06

= Human Factors Concept - Moving into a care home [HFC-02] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-02
|Name|Moving into a care home
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only.
|Concept statement|Moving into a care home means losing a home, a routine and neighbours, often by someone else's decision. Newcomers with dementia take weeks to months to adjust, and many withdraw in that time.
|Human factor addressed|Adjustment; orientation; loneliness
|Proposed mechanism|A newcomer meets many people at once and keeps few names. Expecting to return home, they see little point in getting to know anyone. A few introductions, spread over the first weeks and repeated, leave fewer faces to learn and some to recognise.
|Source|Sury et al. (2013), review: moving in is a time of loss and distress for people with dementia and their families, and few interventions have been tested. Victor (2012): loneliness in care homes.
|Conditions and population of validity|Mostly qualitative studies, across varied homes and countries.
|Predicted Effect type|Newcomers have someone to talk to within weeks, and spend less of the day in their room
|Associated Measures|M-02, M-05, M-06

= Human Factors Concept - Conversation repair [HFC-03] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-03
|Name|Conversation repair
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only.
|Concept statement|Speakers prefer to fix their own slips. In dementia, slips become more frequent and repair succeeds less often.
|Human factor addressed|Conversation; face
|Proposed mechanism|Self-repair needs a moment. Help offered too soon takes the turn and shows the slip. A cue to the last topic, or to a shared one, lets the talk go on. Just after an introduction, a stalled conversation is when two residents give up on each other.
|Source|Schegloff et al. (1977): preference for self-repair. Orange et al. (1996): repair in Alzheimer's disease.
|Conditions and population of validity|Conversation analysis of healthy talk; small samples in Alzheimer's disease.
|Predicted Effect type|Talk after an introduction survives slips, without embarrassment
|Associated Measures|M-04

= Human Factors Concept - Common ground and reminiscence [HFC-04] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-04
|Name|Common ground and reminiscence
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only.
|Concept statement|Conversation rests on what both people know. Old memories outlast recent ones, and a past that two residents share gives them common ground.
|Human factor addressed|Conversation; identity
|Proposed mechanism|Residents rarely discover what they share (PS-03). The System can find it in two consented profiles and mention it once, at the introduction. A topic from early adulthood is recalled with least effort.
|Source|Clark & Brennan (1991): grounding in communication. Rubin et al. (1998): early adulthood is recalled best. Woods et al. (2018), Cochrane review: small, setting-dependent benefits of reminiscence.
|Conditions and population of validity|Reminiscence studied in staff-led groups; benefits small.
|Predicted Effect type|More conversation after an introduction; more positive interaction
|Associated Measures|M-04, M-05

= Human Factors Concept - Theory of mind [HFC-05] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-05
|Name|Theory of mind
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature and group reasoning.
|Concept statement|Understanding what others know, believe and feel weakens in dementia. Two residents often misread each other's interest, discomfort or wish to stop.
|Human factor addressed|Social cognition; consent
|Proposed mechanism|A polite yes is taken for a willing one, and a nod for a lack of interest (PS-03, PS-04). Nobody reads these states reliably, the System included, so it asks each resident privately instead of inferring.
|Source|Bora et al. (2015), meta-analysis: theory of mind impaired in Alzheimer's disease.
|Conditions and population of validity|Measured in laboratory tasks, not in everyday conversation.
|Predicted Effect type|Misreadings between residents; fewer when each is asked privately
|Associated Measures|M-07

= Human Factors Concept - Face and personhood [HFC-06] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-06
|Name|Face and personhood
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only.
|Concept statement|Each interaction affirms or erodes a person's standing. Corrections, quizzing and sing-song speech (elderspeak) erode it.
|Human factor addressed|Experienced dignity; withdrawal
|Proposed mechanism|Help addressed to one person marks them as failing. Addressed to both, as a statement, it marks nobody: "Elena, Jan, good to see you both."
|Source|Goffman (1967): face-work. Kitwood (1997): personhood. Sabat (2001): the self in Alzheimer's disease. Williams et al. (2009): elderspeak provokes resistance.
|Conditions and population of validity|Care-staff speech; robot voices not studied.
|Predicted Effect type|Embarrassment and withdrawal; descriptions of being talked down to
|Associated Measures|M-07, M-09 (code T)

= Human Factors Concept - Coping, withdrawal and loneliness [HFC-07] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-07
|Name|Coping, withdrawal and loneliness
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only.
|Concept statement|Aware of decline, people protect themselves: some carry on as before, some face it openly. Avoiding company protects face and leads to loneliness. Withdrawal is gradual, so others notice it late.
|Human factor addressed|Coping with cognitive loss; loneliness
|Proposed mechanism|Each awkward meeting makes the next one less likely. A first meeting on shared ground, with an easy way out, lowers the cost of trying. Noticing a pattern of staying in lets staff offer company before withdrawal sets in.
|Source|Clare (2003): coping in early Alzheimer's disease. Victor (2012): loneliness in care homes. Holwerda et al. (2014): loneliness predicted dementia onset.
|Conditions and population of validity|Coping studied in early dementia, at home. Loneliness measures in dementia of uncertain validity.
|Predicted Effect type|More willingness to meet others; less loneliness over months
|Associated Measures|M-02, M-08

= Human Factors Concept - Truth and trust [HFC-08] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-08
|Name|Truth and trust
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only.
|Concept statement|Lies told to spare distress are common in dementia care, and contested. Once found out, they cost trust. A blunt truth from a stranger causes grief.
|Human factor addressed|Trust; grief
|Proposed mechanism|Trust builds on consistent, truthful answers from known people. Gentle redirection answers the feeling without saying anything untrue.
|Source|Mental Health Foundation (2016): inquiry on truth and lying in dementia care. James et al. (2006): staff lying, and guidelines.
|Conditions and population of validity|UK care practice; answers given by a System not studied.
|Predicted Effect type|Trust kept; fewer repeated painful questions
|Associated Measures|M-09 (code U), M-10

= Human Factors Concept - Similarity and shared background [HFC-15] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-15
|Name|Similarity and shared background
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only.
|Concept statement|People tend to befriend people like themselves: from the same place, generation or trade, or with the same interests. Residents with dementia still form and keep friendships.
|Human factor addressed|Friendship formation; conversation
|Proposed mechanism|Similarity makes the other person more predictable and gives talk a subject. A shared hometown or trade is often the first thing two strangers look for. With memory loss, residents seldom find it on their own.
|Source|McPherson et al. (2001): review of homophily in social networks. de Medeiros et al. (2012): friendships among people with dementia in long-term care.
|Conditions and population of validity|Homophily established in general populations; untested as a matching rule in care homes.
|Predicted Effect type|Matches on a shared background lead to more repeat contact than chance meetings
|Associated Measures|M-02, M-05

= Human Factors Concept - Fear of rejection and the liking gap [HFC-16] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-16
|Name|Fear of rejection and the liking gap
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only.
|Concept statement|People expect a conversation with a stranger to go worse than it does, and underestimate how much a new acquaintance liked them. So they rarely make the first move, and seldom follow up a good first talk.
|Human factor addressed|Social initiative; friendship formation
|Proposed mechanism|Each side waits for the other (PS-01, PS-03). A third party who asks both privately, and introduces them only when both agree, removes the risk of a "no" that the other hears.
|Source|Epley & Schroeder (2014): commuters expected solitude to be more pleasant, yet enjoyed talking to a stranger more. Boothby et al. (2018): the liking gap persisted after conversations of all lengths and over months.
|Conditions and population of validity|Healthy adults, mostly students and commuters; not tested in dementia.
|Predicted Effect type|More introductions accepted when asked privately; more second meetings
|Associated Measures|M-02, M-07

= Human Factors Concept - The difficulty of saying no [HFC-17] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-17
|Name|The difficulty of saying no
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only.
|Concept statement|Refusing a request face to face is uncomfortable, so people agree to things they would rather not do. They also stay in conversations longer than they want, because ending one feels rude.
|Human factor addressed|Consent; autonomy
|Proposed mechanism|People who ask underestimate how hard refusing is. A private question, with a "no" that reaches nobody, lowers the cost of declining. A meeting with a natural end, and leave to go at any time, lowers the cost of stopping.
|Source|Bohns (2016): requesters underestimate compliance by about half, because they miss how awkward refusing is. Mastroianni et al. (2021): conversations rarely end when both people want them to.
|Conditions and population of validity|Healthy adults, with strangers and acquaintances.
|Predicted Effect type|Polite acceptances followed by avoidance (PS-04); fewer when residents are asked privately
|Associated Measures|M-07, M-09 (code P)

